The issue is that the API calls are being made sequentially using `await` on each call before starting the next one, instead of initiating all calls simultaneously and then awaiting their results.

## Incorrect Code (Sequential):
```javascript
async function fetchData() {
  const data1 = await fetch('https://api1.com/data');
  const data2 = await fetch('https://api2.com/data');
  const data3 = await fetch('https://api3.com/data');

  const result1 = await data1.json();
  const result2 = await data2.json();
  const result3 = await data3.json();

  return { result1, result2, result3 };
}
```

## Correct Code (Parallel):
```javascript
async function fetchData() {
  // Start all fetch operations simultaneously
  const [data1, data2, data3] = await Promise.all([
    fetch('https://api1.com/data'),
    fetch('https://api2.com/data'),
    fetch('https://api3.com/data')
  ]);

  // Parse all responses in parallel
  const [result1, result2, result3] = await Promise.all([
    data1.json(),
    data2.json(),
    data3.json()
  ]);

  return { result1, result2, result3 };
}
```

## Key Difference:
- **Sequential**: Each `await` pauses execution until that specific promise resolves
- **Parallel**: `Promise.all()` initiates all promises simultaneously and waits for all to complete

## Alternative Approach (More concise):
```javascript
async function fetchData() {
  const [result1, result2, result3] = await Promise.all([
    fetch('https://api1.com/data').then(r => r.json()),
    fetch('https://api2.com/data').then(r => r.json()),
    fetch('https://api3.com/data').then(r => r.json())
  ]);

  return { result1, result2, result3 };
}
```

## Benefits of Parallel Execution:
- **Performance**: All API calls happen concurrently, reducing total wait time
- **Efficiency**: Better utilization of network bandwidth
- **Faster results**: Total time ≈ slowest single API call instead of sum of all three

