int maxLeftX = (partitionX == 0) ? Integer.MIN_VALUE : nums1[partitionX - 1];
int maxLeftY = (partitionY == 0) ? Integer.MIN_VALUE : nums2[partitionY - 1];

// Right side minimum
int minRightX = (partitionX == m) ? Integer.MAX_VALUE : nums1[partitionX];
int minRightY = (partitionY == n) ? Integer.MAX_VALUE : nums2[partitionY];
```

The beauty of this approach:
- **`Integer.MIN_VALUE`** ensures that an empty left partition can never violate the `maxLeft <= minRight` condition (since everything on the right will be greater)
- **`Integer.MAX_VALUE`** ensures that an empty right partition can never violate the condition (since everything on the left will be smaller)

This effectively makes the boundary checks implicit in the comparisons rather than requiring separate conditional logic, keeping the code clean and maintainable.

